WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:05.381
Black Panther is finally here, and the hotly
anticipated latest installment in the Marvel

00:00:05.381 --> 00:00:09.790
Cinematic Universe has arrived with a record-breaking
bang at the box office.

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:14.360
The film brings the fictional nation of Wakanda
to life under the expert guidance of director

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:18.670
Ryan Coogler and features a cast of characters
that all make for welcome additions to the

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:19.670
franchise.

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:23.980
The film is a breath of fresh air for superhero
cinema and features a lot of elements that

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:25.990
are instant favorites for Marvel fans.

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:29.910
At the same time, there are some bits of the
picture that are not quite as stellar as the

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:33.839
rest, so let's break down the best and worst
elements of Black Panther.

00:00:33.839 --> 00:00:38.139
You don't have to be as smart as Shuri to
know there are spoilers ahead.

00:00:38.139 --> 00:00:40.739
Best: Wakanda

00:00:40.739 --> 00:00:44.420
Whereas some of the other Marvel films have
treated their locations like set dressing,

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:48.699
Black Panther allows us to fall in love with
Wakanda long before it becomes the site of

00:00:48.699 --> 00:00:50.010
the central skirmish.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:53.460
The home nation of T'Challa is not technically
a character, of course.

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:58.859
But thanks to its citizens, design, and traditions,
Wakanda feels like a real place -- a living,

00:00:58.859 --> 00:01:01.129
breathing nation that we care about.

00:01:01.129 --> 00:01:06.000
Without sending a single Helicarrier crashing
into a building, we feel the real damage caused

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.000
to the nation through the events of the film.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.420
"I'mma burn it all!"

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:13.360
More importantly, we care about that repercussions
of that damage.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:15.750
Worst: The first act

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:21.000
Although Black Panther as a whole is completely
engaging, the first act is a little bit clunky.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.930
In fact, the film doesn't really feel like
it hits its stride until Killmonger arrives

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:26.280
in Wakanda.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:30.530
Once he does, the emotional family dynamics
and rivalry between T'Challa and Killmonger

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:34.390
elevate the the film to a higher level, but
still, it takes a third of the movie to get

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:35.390
there.

00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:39.310
Sure, it's got quite a lot to set up — from
the rules and functions of Wakanda to T'Challa's

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:44.390
various relationships with family and friends
— but it makes for an uneven 45 minutes.

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:48.870
Luckily, however, once the film gets going,
it really gets going.

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:51.190
Best: Something to say

00:01:51.190 --> 00:01:55.290
Most MCU films come down to the question of
"What does it mean to be a hero?"

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:59.440
But Black Panther stands out as one of the
few Marvel films to explore new themes that

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:01.640
strengthen the film's emotional punch.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:05.430
From the get-go, the film sets up a conflict
of tradition versus progression.

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:09.980
The technologically advanced nation keeps
its developments to itself and T'Challa, as

00:02:09.980 --> 00:02:14.340
the new king, finds himself confronted with
the idea that this has caused long-term damage

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:15.739
to the outside world.

00:02:15.739 --> 00:02:20.109
His adversary, Erik Killmonger, serves as
an avatar of these negative repercussions

00:02:20.109 --> 00:02:24.300
and challenges not only T'Challa, but the
very essence of old Wakanda.

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:29.130
The film also explores colonialism and loyalty
to friends and family over country, and it's

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:31.769
all handled immaculately under Coogler's direction.

00:02:31.769 --> 00:02:36.989
He's managed to make the rare superhero film
that is both fun to watch and intellectually

00:02:36.989 --> 00:02:37.989
engaging.

00:02:37.989 --> 00:02:40.730
Worst: What CAN'T vibranium do?

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:45.510
Before Black Panther, the only real exposure
to vibranium we had in the MCU came through

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:49.060
Captain America's shield and the robotic Ultron.

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:53.590
Both were made of the precious metal and were
impenetrably hard and strong as a result.

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:58.190
In Black Panther, however, we not only see
more vibranium than was previously imaginable,

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:02.299
but we learn that it's the basis of everything
that makes Wakanda such a force, from those

00:03:02.299 --> 00:03:05.950
snazzy communication devices to the sophisticated
monorail system.

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:09.740
At some point you've got to step back and
wonder if there's anything this new element

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:11.090
can't do.

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:15.159
Very little of the writing in Black Panther
comes off as lazy, but the fact that vibranium

00:03:15.159 --> 00:03:21.189
is a catch-all that can serve every plot device
does seem a little too convenient at times.

00:03:21.189 --> 00:03:23.609
Best: An engaging villain

00:03:23.609 --> 00:03:26.779
It shouldn't come as a surprise to anyone
who's been paying attention to his career

00:03:26.779 --> 00:03:30.409
but Michael B. Jordan is excellent in Black
Panther.

00:03:30.409 --> 00:03:34.980
From The Wire to Creed, Jordan has become
one of the most engaging, magnetic actors

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:39.109
working today, and he delivers one of the
most compelling villainous performances in

00:03:39.109 --> 00:03:40.659
the MCU to-date.

00:03:40.659 --> 00:03:43.930
Of course, it didn't hurt that he was given
good material to work with.

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:48.590
Erik Killmonger is a great villain because
his perspective isn't completely wrong.

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:52.499
He believes in his birthright to the throne
and avenging his late father, and he wants

00:03:52.499 --> 00:03:56.870
Wakanda to share its treasures with the world
so that racial oppression can come to an end.

00:03:56.870 --> 00:04:01.379
If it weren't for his call for merciless violence
and his desire to rule the world, well, it'd

00:04:01.379 --> 00:04:03.590
be pretty hard to argue with his rationale.

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:05.909
Worst: Everett Ross

00:04:05.909 --> 00:04:09.900
It's common for Marvel films to feature characters
that serve as connective tissue between the

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:14.629
realms we know and those that are all-new,
which is why CIA Agent Everett Ross comes

00:04:14.629 --> 00:04:16.510
into the picture for Black Panther.

00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:20.060
The problem with him, though, is that the
nation has already been established without

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:24.320
him by the time he shows up in the country,
and he doesn't have a particularly compelling

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:25.320
arc either.

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:29.660
Sure, he steps up to help stop Killmonger's
weapons transfer in the end, but he hardly

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:32.070
sacrifices anything in the process.

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:36.780
As talented as Martin Freeman may be, the
character never ends up living up to his potential

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:39.940
and ends up sucking the life from most scenes
he's in.

00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:42.390
Best: Women of Wakanda

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:47.060
As great as Chadwick Boseman is as the title
hero, the women of Wakanda outshine him at

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:49.090
almost every chance.

00:04:49.090 --> 00:04:53.810
The characters Shuri , Nakia, and Okoye are
so talented and engrossing that they steal

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:55.060
every scene.

00:04:55.060 --> 00:05:00.300
From Okoye's unwavering voice of reason and
supreme combat skills to Nakia's brute determination

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:05.400
and sense of morality to Shuri's wit and innovation,
the ladies of Black Panther downright made

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:06.880
the movie what it was.

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:08.160
"Show off."

00:05:08.650 --> 00:05:12.160
Worst: A final battle that gives us deja vu

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:16.900
Black Panther is refreshing in its refusal
to recycle formulas, tropes, and story structures

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:18.930
so often utilized by superhero movies.

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:24.130
Unfortunately, though, it does lean hard on
one groan-worthy tradition: having the hero

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:26.080
fight someone in a similar suit.

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:30.190
In the end, Erik sports his own version of
the Black Panther costume -- just as the villains

00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:33.280
did in the first two Iron Man movies, Ant-Man,
and more.

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:37.680
You can argue that Killmonger wearing the
armor emphasizes the fact that he's a mirror

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:42.370
image of T'Challa, but it's still the same
metaphor that so many movies before it tapped

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:45.220
into, so it seems a little trite at this point.

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:48.270
Best: The second-act twist

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:51.940
Unlike most of its predecessors, there's a
significant portion of Black Panther that

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:54.100
carries on without its protagonist.

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:57.991
Sure, T'Challa doesn't actually die in his
challenge bout with Killmonger, but he does

00:05:57.991 --> 00:06:02.940
come awfully close, which leaves his family
and friends to fight for Wakanda without him.

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:06.940
Marvel films take a great deal of inspiration
from the Hero's Journey template, which requires

00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:08.530
that the hero be present.

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:12.150
But Black Panther breaks from this norm by
taking the time to show the audience what

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:15.050
happens in the wake of T'Challa's apparent
demise.

00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:19.120
It's easily the strongest chunk of the movie,
giving a depth to the story and its characters

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:21.970
that no Marvel movie has ever achieved.

00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:25.090
Worst: The first post-credits scene

00:06:25.090 --> 00:06:29.220
The first post-credits scene in Black Panther
features T'Challa formally announcing to the

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:32.370
world that Wakanda will be doing away with
its isolationism.

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:36.060
The scene might draw a smile when a diplomat
questions what good a third-world country

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:39.990
could offer anyone, but the reality is, the
clip is redundant.

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:44.210
The film's proper ending already establishes
the opening of a Wakandan Outreach Center,

00:06:44.210 --> 00:06:48.930
so other than allowing T'Challa to get the
last laugh, it serves very little purpose.

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:53.030
Considering Marvel's post-credit scenes usually
either make us laugh or introduce something

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:56.990
new, Black Panther's bonus scene is a rare
miss.

00:06:56.990 --> 00:06:57.990
Thanks for watching!

